xitem(tt(zdraw) tt(snapshot) var(targetwin) var(association) [ tt(occupancy) ])
xitem(tt(zdraw) tt(copy) var(sourcewin) var(source_row) var(source_column) var(targetwin) var(row) var(column) var(rows) var(columns))
xitem(tt(zdraw) tt(overlay) var(sourcewin) var(source_row) var(source_column) var(targetwin) var(row) var(column) var(rows) var(columns))
xitem(tt(zdraw) tt(restyle) var(targetwin) var(row) var(column) var(rows) var(columns) var(style))
xitem(tt(zdraw) tt(fill) var(targetwin) var(row) var(column) var(rows) var(columns) var(style) var(tile))
item(tt(zdraw) tt(resize) var(height) var(width) [ tt(endwin) | tt(nosave) | tt(endwin_nosave) ])(
Manipulate curses windows. Except for tt(geometry), tt(colorinfo), tt(resourceinfo), tt(textinfo),
tt(textpos), tt(textwrap), tt(capabilities) and the headless tt(raster)
operations described below, uses of
this command should be
bracketed by `tt(zdraw init)' to initialise use of curses, and
`tt(zdraw end)' to end it; omitting `tt(zdraw end)' can cause
the terminal to be in an unwanted state.

The subcommand tt(addwin) creates a window with var(nlines) lines and
var(ncols) columns.  Its upper left corner will be placed at row
var(begin_y) and column
var(begin_x) of the screen.  var(targetwin) is a string and refers
to the name of a window that is not currently assigned.  Note
in particular the curses convention that vertical values appear
before horizontal values.

If tt(addwin) is given an existing window as the final argument, the new
window is created as a subwindow of var(parentwin).  This differs from an
ordinary new window in that the memory of the window contents is shared
with the parent's memory.  Subwindows must be deleted before their parent.
Note that the coordinates of subwindows are relative to the screen, not
the parent, as with other windows.

Use the subcommand tt(delwin) to delete a window created with
tt(addwin) or tt(addpad). The tt(end) subcommand releases registered windows
and pads. Deleting a window does not erase its existing screen image.

The window corresponding to the full visible screen is called
tt(stdscr); it always exists after `tt(zdraw init)' and cannot
be deleted with tt(delwin).

The subcommand tt(refresh) will refresh window var(targetwin); this is
necessary to make any pending changes (such as characters you have
prepared for output with tt(char)) visible on the screen.  tt(refresh)
without an argument causes the screen to be cleared and redrawn.
If multiple windows are given, the screen is updated once at the end.

The subcommand tt(touch) marks the var(targetwin)s listed as changed.
This is necessary before tt(refresh)ing windows if a window that
was in front of another window (which may be tt(stdscr)) is deleted.

The subcommand tt(movewin) moves an ordinary window's screen origin to
zero-based nonnegative decimal coordinates, preserving its dimensions, retained
cells, logical cursor and drawing/input settings. It differs from tt(move),
which moves the cursor inside a surface. The whole window must fit in the
current curses screen.

The subcommand tt(resizewin) takes positive literal decimal dimensions and an
optional pair of screen coordinates. Both optional coordinates must be supplied
together; omitting them preserves the origin. The resulting rectangle must fit
inside the current curses screen. Both old and requested window areas are
limited to 262144 cells; requested dimensions are each limited to 32767.
Use terminal tt(resize) with tt(nosave) to update screen dimensions first.

Both operations accept only independent ordinary windows without children.
They reject tt(stdscr), pads, subwindows and windows with subwindows. Shared
storage relationships require separate handling. Use tt(resizepad) for pads.

Resizing retains the upper-left overlap subject to native wide-edge repair.
New cells use the existing background character and its attributes, not the
current drawing style. Shrunk-away cells are discarded; subsequent growth does
not restore them. The cursor is clamped independently to the last valid row
and column when needed. Current attributes, full color pair, background, input
timeout and scrolling mode are retained, including existing RGB state after
tt(truecolor off).

A private duplicate is resized and positioned before replacing the live window.
Recoverable allocation, resize, move or setup failures leave the original handle
and retained contents intact. Failure to release the original also retains that
handle. Limits describe cell areas, not exact peak memory: duplicate/reallocation
storage is needed in addition to the original. The handle name and registry
position are preserved.

Neither operation stages, reads input or presents. Previously queued screen
cells stay in place. Recompose the old footprint with a background, stage the
updated window and call tt(present). Ordinary input and inherited tt(refresh)
keep their usual presentation behavior. Use no-refresh input where supported
for an explicit application presentation boundary.

The linked library's tt(wresize) behavior handles wide characters intersecting
a new right edge. No independent Unicode reconstruction is provided; align cuts
to complete characters where possible. Redraw layout-dependent content such as
borders after resizing.

Check tt(window_movement) for tt(mvwin) support. tt(window_resize) requires
 tt(wresize), tt(mvwin), tt(wattr_get) and tt(wattr_set). Explicit style restoration
preserves high color-pair IDs that some tt(dupwin) implementations lose.
Both commands require tt(init). Status is 0 on success, 1 for invalid arguments,
surface relationships, bounds, resource limits or library errors, and 2 for
missing compiled support.

The subcommand tt(treewin) reshapes one member of a shared window tree by
rebuilding the entire owning tree. Its dimensions are positive literal decimal
integers and its coordinates are absolute, nonnegative screen coordinates.
The root must be an owned ordinary window; pads, tt(stdscr), and trees rooted in
tt(stdscr) are excluded. Independent tt(movewin)/tt(resizewin) behavior is unchanged.
Every result must fit the current curses screen, and each child must fit its
immediate parent. Descendants of a moved target retain their relative offsets
and sizes; other siblings keep their geometry. There is no clipping, scaling or
reparenting. Grow parents before children; shrink descendants before ancestors.

Root resize retains overlapping backing cells and fills new cells with the root
background, using native wide-edge behavior. Shrinking discards cells. Moving or
resizing a child selects a different view of shared parent cells; its former text
does not move with it. Child backgrounds remain drawing defaults. Each window
retains its name, registry position, attributes/pair, background, scroll setting
and timeout, with its own cursor clamped to the new dimensions. No input, staging
or presentation occurs. Recompose old footprints explicitly. Arbitrary terminal
resize can alter native window geometry; inspect and repair valid trees or recreate
them from application state after drastic shrink.

The limit is 64 windows per tree, target dimensions at most 32767, and old/new areas at
most 262144 cells per window. Collection is iterative. A private backing copy and
parent-first shared views are prepared before live handles change. Validation,
allocation and setup failures leave the live tree intact. Zsh traps are deferred
during construction, publication and retirement. Old children are freed before
parents. A retirement error returns 1 with a warning that new geometry was applied:
live handles remain coherent and one bounded retired tree is retained for cleanup.
Another tt(treewin) retries cleanup before allocating and refuses a rebuild if
cleanup still fails. Failed candidate cleanup is retained similarly without
changing live handles. tt(end)/unload make a best-effort attempt; persistent library
deletion errors cannot guarantee reclamation. These limits are not exact RSS bounds.

Check tt(window_trees), requiring independent resize support plus tt(wgetbkgrnd)
and tt(wbkgrndset). Status is 0 on complete success, 1 for state/bounds/resources
or library failure, and 2 without compiled support. Initialization is required.

The subcommand tt(addpad) creates a blank offscreen surface in the window
namespace. Pads appear in tt(zdraw_windows) and can exceed the terminal size.
Dimensions are positive literal decimal integers, at most 32767 each. A pad is
limited to 262144 cells; live public pads share a limit of 1048576 cells.
Failed validation or allocation does not register a handle or consume budget.
These are cell limits, not exact memory quotas; ordinary windows, prepared rows,
snapshots and private temporary pads have separate storage. Pads retain all
allocated cells and do not provide automatic dataset virtualization.

Drawing and inspection commands accept pads in their own coordinates, including
prepared rows, tt(fill), tt(copy), tt(restyle) and tt(snapshot). Existing limits
of those operations remain in force. In the six-field tt(position) array, a
pad's screen-origin fields are -1, -1; cursor and dimensions describe the pad.
Pads reject tt(input), tt(event), tt(timeout), ordinary tt(refresh), and tt(stage).
Use an ordinary window for input. tt(addwin) rejects pad parents; subpads are
not exposed. Pad dimensions remain fixed across terminal tt(resize).

The subcommand tt(resizepad) changes a public pad's allocated dimensions,
preserving its handle name and registry position. Ordinary windows and tt(stdscr)
are rejected. Dimensions use the same positive literal decimal format and limits
as tt(addpad). The old allocation is credited against the session cell budget:
a same-area resize can succeed at the full budget, and successful shrinkage
releases cells for other pads. The pad can exceed the terminal dimensions.

The upper-left overlap survives subject to native wide-character edge repair.
New cells use the existing background character and attributes, not the current
drawing style. Shrinking discards cells permanently; growth does not restore
them. The cursor is clamped independently to the last valid row and column.
Current attributes, full color pair, complex background and scrolling mode are
preserved, including existing RGB state after tt(truecolor off). No new pairs
are allocated.

A private genuine pad receives the original cells and background before native
tt(wresize) changes its dimensions. The original is released and its registered
pointer and accounting replaced only after preparation succeeds. Allocation,
copying, resize, setup and original-release failures retain the original pad and
budget. Temporary pad/reallocation storage is additional to live cell limits;
these are not exact peak-memory bounds. The public tt(copy) operation's smaller
cell limit does not restrict tt(resizepad).

Resizing does not stage, read input or present. Previously queued screen cells
survive even if their source rows are truncated. Select valid viewport bounds,
recompose backgrounds and affected surfaces, then call tt(present). No viewport
mapping is retained or adjusted. Resized pads keep their input restrictions.
The linked library's tt(wresize) repair behavior applies at a clipped wide
character; align cuts to complete characters and redraw dependent content where
needed. There is no independent Unicode reconstruction.

Check tt(pad_resize) for pad support plus tt(copywin), tt(wresize), tt(wgetbkgrnd),
tt(wbkgrndset), tt(wattr_get) and tt(wattr_set). The command requires tt(init).
Status is 0 on success, 1 for invalid arguments, limits or library failure,
and 2 for missing compiled support.

The subcommand tt(viewport) stages a rectangular pad view at screen coordinates.
Coordinates are zero-based nonnegative decimal integers and dimensions are
positive decimal integers. Both rectangles must fit completely in the pad and
current curses screen. There is no clipping, expansion or negative-coordinate
normalization. Handle terminal resize events and update curses' screen dimensions
before selecting a new viewport. The module stores no viewport mapping for
automatic redisplay. Staging does not modify the retained pad cells or cursor.

The subcommand tt(stage) stages complete ordinary windows in argument order,
validating the whole list first. Both tt(stage) and tt(viewport) mark contributing
rows for copying, so unchanged surfaces can cover earlier contributions.
Later contributions determine overlapping composed cells, subject to curses'
wide-character behavior. Multiple views of one pad can contribute to a frame.
Moving a view does not erase its old location; stage a background there.

The subcommand tt(present) updates the terminal from the composed virtual screen
without staging another surface. Neither tt(stage) nor tt(viewport) presents
by itself. Their dirty markers and virtual-screen state change, but retained
text, drawing styles, backgrounds and logical cursors do not. Physical cursor
placement follows curses' composition rules. Frames are not implicitly reset;
repeated tt(present) calls use the current virtual screen and curses screen diff.

Inherited tt(refresh) remains available for ordinary windows and also presents
queued contributions. Bare tt(refresh) adds tt(stdscr) first and can cover a
viewport. Default input may refresh its window; use tt(event) with tt(norefresh)
when supported for explicit presentation. No synchronized-output protocol or
atomic terminal-paint guarantee is implied by ordinary staging/presentation.

The optional subcommand tt(sync on) configures synchronized output for subsequent
tt(present) calls. It requires an accepted tt(synchronized_output) query report
of tt(reset) (mode 2026, report 2). Unknown, unrecognized, already-set and permanent
states are insufficient; there is no force form. Capability overrides affect
inspection only. Activation itself writes nothing, reads no input and sends no
query. Repeated on/off calls are idempotent. tt(sync off) clears configuration and
retries any outstanding reset. Initialization is required. Status is 0 for success,
1 for invalid arguments/state or library/I/O failure, and 2 without compiled
support or sufficient evidence. Check tt(synchronized_output) in tt(zdraw_features).

Configured tt(present) first retries any outstanding reset, then queues Zsh signal
traps, flushes preceding stdout, writes and flushes tt(CSI ? 2026 h), calls
tt(doupdate) once, and flushes output. It attempts tt(CSI ? 2026 l) and a final
flush even after a failed begin or update, before releasing its guard and deferred
traps. Nested attempts fail. Empty updates still emit a balanced pair (16 bytes).
There is no callback, input wait or open begin/end interval across successful shell
commands. Ordinary tt(refresh), implicit input refreshes and tt(resume) repaint
remain unsynchronized. Use consistent explicit frame boundaries where required.

The region is bounded to one update and marker writes, not a hard wall-clock
deadline. Blocking curses/stdio writes, process suspension, SIGKILL or a failed
transport can delay or prevent reset. There is no universal protocol timeout,
watchdog, replacement signal trap or forced nonblocking descriptor mode. A failed
reset retains a cleanup obligation for tt(present), tt(sync off), tt(suspend),
tt(end) or unload to retry. Reset is idempotent; I/O failure cannot establish what
the peer applied. End/unload make a best-effort attempt and clear state. Failed
presentation does not roll back curses' screen models or promise atomic painting.

Suspend retains configuration with no open region; resume repaints normally and
the next explicit tt(present) uses synchronization again. End/unload clear the
setting and evidence. The capability tt(enabled) field describes configured
presentation in an active session, not a permanently enabled terminal mode;
it is tt(no) while suspended and after off/end/unload.

Use tt(delwin) to release a pad and its budget. Deletion does not erase or cancel
previously staged cells. Failed pad deletion retains its handle and budget for
retry. tt(end) and module unload release pads through normal window cleanup.
No extra input owner or terminal protocol is introduced.

Align viewport edges to complete wide characters where possible. Native curses
clipping and overlap behavior applies; there is no glyph reconstruction or
portable repair guarantee for split wide characters.

Check tt(offscreen_pads) for compiled tt(newpad) and tt(pnoutrefresh) support,
and tt(staged_refresh) for tt(stage) and tt(present). All four commands require
tt(init). Status is 0 on success and 1 for invalid input, limits, allocation or
library errors. tt(addpad) and tt(viewport) return 2 without compiled pad support.
Validation failures leave queued cells unchanged. Library staging failures can
leave partial queued contributions; presentation failure can leave partial
terminal output. No rollback is provided.

The subcommand tt(move) moves the cursor position in var(targetwin) to
new coordinates var(new_y) and var(new_x).  Note that the 
subcommand tt(string) (but not the subcommand tt(char)) advances the
cursor position over the characters added.

The subcommand tt(clear) erases the contents of var(targetwin).  One
(and no more than one) of three options may be specified.  With the
option tt(redraw), in addition the next tt(refresh) of var(targetwin)
will cause the screen to be cleared and repainted.  With the option
tt(eol), var(targetwin) is only cleared to the end of the current cursor
line.  With the option
tt(bot), var(targetwin) is cleared to the end of the window, i.e
everything to the right and below the cursor is cleared.

The subcommand tt(geometry) writes the current size of the controlling
terminal into the array named var(array), as two decimal integers: rows,
then columns.  It queries the terminal directly, so the result can differ
from the dimensions cached by curses before a resize has been processed.
It does not resize windows, refresh the screen, or change terminal modes,
and can be used before tt(init) and after tt(end).
The return status is 0 on success, 1 if the terminal size cannot be obtained,
either dimension is zero, or the parameter cannot be assigned, and 2 if
terminal size queries are not supported by the build.  A failed terminal
query leaves var(array) unchanged.  No fallback dimensions are invented.

The subcommand tt(colorinfo) replaces the ordinary writable associative
parameter named var(association) with runtime color information.  An absent
parameter is created; readonly parameters, special parameters, other parameter
types and subscripted names are rejected.  There is no default output parameter.
Return status 0 means the information was assigned, including when curses has
not been initialized; status 1 indicates invalid arguments or assignment failure.

This query works before tt(init) and after tt(end).  It does not initialize
curses, refresh windows, allocate color pairs, read terminal input, emit terminal
output or negotiate terminal protocols.  It reads the module's current session
state and curses' cached limits.  The existing tt(ZDRAW_COLORS) and
tt(ZDRAW_COLOR_PAIRS) parameters are unchanged.

The following keys are returned; their order is unspecified and callers should
ignore unfamiliar future keys.  Values are decimal integers or tt(unknown).
Before tt(init) and after tt(end), tt(initialized) is 0 and every other value
below is tt(unknown).  During a session the following meanings apply:
startsitem()
sitem(tt(initialized))(1 when the module has an active curses session.)
sitem(tt(has_colors))(0 or 1: curses' color capability for the terminal type.)
sitem(tt(color_started))(0 or 1: whether tt(start_color) succeeded during initialization.)
sitem(tt(default_colors))(0 or 1: whether tt(use_default_colors) succeeded during
initialization.  This is 0 if it is not compiled in or color initialization
failed; it describes support for mixing tt(default) with other colors.)
sitem(tt(can_change_color))(0 or 1: curses' palette-redefinition capability.
This does not promise direct RGB drawing or expose a palette modification command.)
sitem(tt(truecolor_supported))(0 or 1: the compiled library and initialized
terminal description support the RGB interface described below.)
sitem(tt(truecolor_enabled))(0 or 1: whether the application has enabled RGB
color arguments in this session.)
sitem(tt(rgb_min), tt(rgb_max))(The inclusive packed RGB range; tt(unknown)
when unsupported.  These are separate from the decimal-index tt(color_limit).)
sitem(tt(colors))(The raw curses color count after successful tt(start_color);
otherwise tt(unknown).)
sitem(tt(color_pairs))(The raw curses pair count after successful tt(start_color);
otherwise tt(unknown).  This includes reserved pair 0.)
sitem(tt(color_limit))(The number of numeric color indices representable through
the module: the smaller of tt(colors) and tt(SHRT_MAX+1).  Valid indices start
at 0.  This is 0 if color initialization failed.)
sitem(tt(pair_limit))(The maximum number of nonzero pair IDs the module can
allocate: the smaller of tt(color_pairs-1) and tt(SHRT_MAX), bounded below by 0.
This is also the highest allocatable pair ID, and is 0 if color initialization failed.)
sitem(tt(bg_pair_limit))(The highest pair ID representable by tt(bg), no greater
than tt(pair_limit).  Narrow curses paths have an additional packed-attribute
limit and the existing maximum of 255.)
sitem(tt(query_pair_limit))(The highest pair ID representable by tt(querychar),
no greater than tt(pair_limit).  Narrow readback has a packed-attribute limit.)
sitem(tt(spans_pair_limit))(The highest pair ID representable by tt(spans),
no greater than tt(pair_limit).  The ASCII path has an additional packed-attribute
limit.  This is 0 when span drawing is unavailable.)
sitem(tt(pairs_used))(The number of allocated nonzero pair IDs in this session.)
sitem(tt(pairs_free))(tt(pair_limit) minus tt(pairs_used).)
endsitem()

Limits are upper bounds, not reservations or guarantees that a drawing call
will succeed.  Successful tt(start_color) can initialize a table of zero colors
on a monochrome terminal.  Pair accounting follows existing allocation behavior: different
spellings may consume separate pairs, deleting a window does not free pairs,
and tt(end) clears allocations.  Limits for tt(bg) and tt(querychar) concern
the assigned pair ID, not a foreground or background color index.  The first
use of tt(default/default) retains the existing allocation behavior.
Capabilities reflect curses' terminal description, not a probe of the physical
terminal.  Compiled support remains separately discoverable through
tt(zdraw_features), including its tt(colorinfo) entry.

The subcommand tt(resourceinfo) creates or replaces an ordinary writable
associative parameter, following tt(colorinfo)'s destination rules. It is available
before tt(init), after tt(end), without a controlling terminal or tt(TERM), and
while suspended. It does not read input, activate protocols, touch or refresh
windows, allocate surfaces, or retry failed cleanup. Its fields are:

startsitem()
sitem(tt(format), tt(session))(The format is tt(zdraw-resources-1); session is
one of tt(inactive), tt(active), or tt(suspended).)
sitem(tt(windows), tt(owned_windows), tt(child_windows))(Public non-pad windows
including tt(stdscr); independent application windows excluding tt(stdscr); and
shared subwindows at every depth, respectively.)
sitem(tt(window_cells), tt(backing_cells))(Sum of public non-pad window areas,
including overlapping children; and sum of root areas including tt(stdscr) but
excluding children. Neither estimates curses allocations or resident memory.)
sitem(tt(pads), tt(pad_cells), tt(private_input_pads))(Public pad count, budgeted
public pad area, and zero or one private one-cell input pad.)
sitem(tt(retired_tree_windows))(Non-null handles retained after failed tree
cleanup. Inspection leaves them intact; repeated cleanup failure can retain
handles even after tt(end). Shared handles are not independent allocations.)
sitem(tt(cached_color_pairs))(Live module color-pair cache entries. Allocator
limits are separately available from tt(colorinfo).)
sitem(tt(prepared_rows), tt(prepared_bytes))(Live prepared rows and their
accounted storage, following tt(rowinfo)'s accounting.)
sitem(tt(prepared_created), tt(prepared_draws))(Successful preparations and
successful tt(draw) calls since session start, including subsequently released
rows and empty or zero-budget draws. Failures do not count, even if a failed
write changed some cells. Counters survive suspension and reset at tt(end).)
sitem(tt(counter_limit))(The tt(ZLONG_MAX) saturation limit for cumulative
counters and window totals.)
sitem(tt(prepared_byte_limit))(16 MiB for supported prepared rows. Unsupported
builds report tt(unknown), with all prepared counts and bytes zero.)
sitem(tt(pad_cell_limit), tt(pad_total_cell_limit), tt(pad_dimension_limit))(
262144 cells per public pad, 1048576 total cells, and 32767 per dimension.)
sitem(tt(resize_cell_limit), tt(resize_dimension_limit), tt(tree_window_limit))(
262144 cells per old/new window, 32767 per requested dimension, and 64 handles
per reconstructed tree. At most one bounded retired tree is retained.)
sitem(tt(copy_cell_limit), tt(snapshot_cell_limit), tt(snapshot_byte_limit))(
65536 cells per copy/overlay rectangle; 65536 snapshot cells; and 16 MiB of
snapshot key/value strings.)
endsitem()

Except for the prepared byte limit, budgets describe configured limits even if
an optional operation is unavailable. Check tt(zdraw_features) for support. They
do not guarantee allocation success or add limits to inherited tt(addwin).
The query's own result storage, allocator/hash-table overhead, private curses
screen buffers and shell-owned companion data are not included. No elapsed time
or emulator paint-time estimate is produced by this query.

The subcommand tt(textinfo) measures var(text) and optionally clips it to a
prefix fitting within var(columns).  It can be used before tt(init), after
tt(end), and without a controlling terminal or tt(TERM).  It replaces or creates
an ordinary writable associative parameter named var(association).  Readonly or
special parameters, subscripts and other parameter types are rejected.  Omitting
var(columns) returns and measures all text.  The result has these keys:
startsitem()
sitem(tt(text))(The retained prefix, preserving its original bytes.)
sitem(tt(remainder))(The omitted suffix; concatenating this with the prefix
reconstructs the input.)
sitem(tt(width))(The prefix width in columns.)
sitem(tt(total_width))(The entire input width in columns.)
sitem(tt(truncated))(1 if any text was omitted, otherwise 0.)
endsitem()

A clipping unit is one positive-width character followed by all immediately
following zero-width characters.  The system tt(wcwidth) function and current
locale determine widths; Zsh's optional Unicode width table is not used.
Multibyte characters are never split.  A unit is retained if its positive-width
character fits; all its zero-width characters stay with it.  The first non-fitting
unit ends the prefix, even if a later narrower character could fit in the unused
space.  No padding, ellipsis, replacement character or normalization is added.

Text must be printable, and either empty or begin with a positive-width
character.  Controls, tabs, line breaks, NULs, invalid encoding and leading
zero-width characters are rejected.  The entire input is validated, including
the omitted suffix.  Budgets are unsigned decimal integers from 0 through
tt(INT_MAX); an input whose total width exceeds tt(INT_MAX) is rejected.
A zero budget is valid.  Invalid input leaves an existing result unchanged and
does not create a missing parameter.  The return status is 0 on successful
measurement or clipping, 1 on invalid input or assignment failure, and 2 for
non-ASCII text on a build without multibyte text support.

The compiled features tt(textinfo) and tt(wide_text) describe availability.
Printable ASCII is always supported.  Multibyte text requires tt(wide_text),
a suitable locale and the tt(MULTIBYTE) option.  On such a build, an unsuitable
locale or tt(MULTIBYTE) being unset causes non-ASCII text to fail with status 1.
Measurement is independent of wide curses support and does not enforce curses'
complex-character storage limit.  The query does not initialize curses, allocate
colors, read input, change terminal modes or emit terminal output.

The subcommand tt(textpos) takes an association, text, the literal tt(column) or
 tt(byte), and a nonnegative decimal offset. It returns the complete clipping
unit containing that position, using the same width and validation rules as
 tt(textinfo). Either column of a double-width character selects the whole unit.
Any byte in a multibyte character or its following zero-width characters selects
that same unit. All offsets are zero-based and range ends are exclusive. Byte
offsets count the original encoded bytes, not shell character indices or internal
escapes. The ordinary writable association is replaced or created with:
startsitem()
sitem(tt(text))(The complete selected clipping unit, preserving original bytes.)
sitem(tt(prefix), tt(remainder))(Text before and after the unit. Concatenating
 tt(prefix), tt(text) and tt(remainder) reconstructs the entire input.)
sitem(tt(byte_start), tt(byte_end))(Source byte range.)
sitem(tt(column_start), tt(column_end))(Displayed column range.)
sitem(tt(total_bytes), tt(total_width))(Entire input byte length and column width.)
sitem(tt(at_end))(1 for the end boundary, otherwise 0.)
endsitem()

An offset equal to the chosen total selects an empty range at the end, with
all input in tt(prefix). Offset zero is therefore valid for empty text. Offsets
past the end fail. Offsets, byte length and width must fit in tt(INT_MAX).
Decimal offsets are parsed literally, without shell evaluation. All input is
validated before assignment, including the suffix after a hit. Invalid input
leaves an existing result unchanged and does not create an absent target.
Return status is 0 on success, 1 on invalid input or assignment failure, and 2
for non-ASCII text on a build without tt(wide_text).

Check tt(text_positions) for compiled support. This query requires no initialized
session or terminal and has no terminal or input side effects. It scans the
input once per query and returns one range without constructing a full map.
Results depend on the current locale and tt(MULTIBYTE) option. Recompute after
changing those or the text. The default units are not Unicode grapheme clusters: a
zero-width joiner stays with its preceding base and the next positive-width
character starts another unit. Terminal emoji shaping and curses storage limits
are not modeled. To hit-test clipped text, query the prefix from tt(textinfo)
and subtract the drawing origin from the mouse's screen coordinates.

The optional final argument tt(grapheme) to tt(textpos), or after an explicit
column budget to tt(textinfo), selects a terminal-oriented Unicode 17.0.0
extended grapheme profile. Omitting it or specifying tt(cell) preserves the
existing boundaries and result fields. Successful tt(grapheme) results also
include tt(policy) equal to tt(grapheme) and tt(unicode_version) equal to
 tt(17.0.0). Entire groups of emoji modifiers, regional indicators, pictographic
joiners, Hangul and Indic conjuncts are selected or clipped together. Source
bytes are preserved, and column widths still sum system tt(wcwidth) values.

The engine implements UAX #29 revision 47. The terminal profile suppresses a
boundary before any system-zero-width character, preserving existing native
clipping units. It is therefore a declared profile, not an exact unmodified
extended-grapheme boundary query. Existing printable-text validation, including
rejection of initial zero-width characters, remains. Segmentation does not imply
correct terminal shaping, compatible emoji advances or sufficient curses storage.
Native drawing operations and tt(textwrap) retain their original contracts.

Check tt(grapheme_boundaries) for compiled support. The explicit policy requires
multibyte support, tt(nl_langinfo) with tt(CODESET), an active UTF-8 codeset and the
 tt(MULTIBYTE) option; otherwise it returns 2 without replacing the result, even
for ASCII input. Unknown policy names return 2. Input over 1048576 original
bytes returns 1. All input is validated before assignment. The policy is per call;
no terminal probing, locale changes or session state are introduced. The static
property data is shipped with the module under the Unicode data license, without
a new runtime library or network dependency. Segmentation retains constant-sized
suffix state and searches the property ranges per decoded character.

The explicit policy tt(unicode-17.0.0-egc-wcwidth-sum-attach-zero) shares the
same Unicode boundary profile and summed libc widths with native drawing.
It additionally validates every spacing-scalar/zero-width-suffix native group
by an exact tt(setcchar)/tt(getcchar) round-trip, including clipped suffixes.
Groups exceeding native storage fail with status 2 instead of silently losing
text. Query results include the complete policy name and Unicode version.
No emoji width override is applied: woman + ZWJ + laptop occupies four native
cells where the scalar widths are 2, 0 and 2; adding X advances five cells.

Pass this policy as the final query argument, as tt(policy=)var(policy) before
the first style/text pair in tt(spans) or tt(spansclip), or after the text in
 tt(string). Spans are segmented together, but each span must contain complete
encoded scalars. All native cells in a grapheme use the complete style of its
first scalar. Later styles are checked but take effect only at a new grapheme.
Clipping omits an entire first non-fitting grapheme and its suffix; the window
edge is also a budget. All source text and styles are validated before writing.
The per-call limits are 1048576 original text bytes and 4096 span pairs.

Safe tt(string) preflights a printable single-row write and advances by its
measured width. An exact row-end advances to the next row; an ending at the
bottom-right fails before writing. Excess text never wraps or scrolls. Spaces
are literal cells, using current attributes and color without background
character substitution. Current style and background are preserved. Spans
continue preserving the cursor. Omitting the policy retains inherited behavior;
 tt(cell) or tt(libc-wcwidth) explicitly selects that behavior.

The headless subcommand tt(textpolicy) writes an ordinary writable association,
or creates an absent one, with the following fields:
startsitem()
sitem(tt(policy), tt(default_policy))(Selected name; the default is tt(libc-wcwidth).)
sitem(tt(grapheme_policy))(The complete safe policy name, even when unavailable.)
sitem(tt(grapheme_compiled), tt(grapheme_available))(Whether the complete path is
compiled, and whether it is also usable with the current locale/options.)
sitem(tt(width_model), tt(locale))(tt(sum-libc-wcwidth) and current tt(LC_CTYPE).)
sitem(tt(boundaries), tt(unicode_version))(Safe: tt(unicode-egc-attach-zero) and
 tt(17.0.0); legacy: tt(spacing-attach-zero) and tt(none).)
sitem(tt(style_policy))(Safe: tt(first-scalar); legacy spans: tt(per-span).)
sitem(tt(native_storage_checked))(1 for the safe policy, otherwise 0.)
sitem(tt(emoji_two_cells), tt(intra_grapheme_styles), tt(persistent_grapheme_metadata))(All 0.)
sitem(tt(operations))(tt(textinfo textpos spans spansclip string).)
sitem(tt(max_bytes), tt(max_spans))(Safe: 1048576 and 4096; legacy: -1.)
sitem(tt(native_cell_scalar_limit))(Native scalar capacity excluding terminator,
or -1 without the complete backend. A grapheme can contain multiple native cells.)
endsitem()

Without a policy argument, discovery reports the default and safe-policy
availability. An explicit unsupported request returns 2 without assignment;
the boundary-only tt(grapheme) alias is not a complete drawing policy and is
rejected by tt(textpolicy) and drawing. Check tt(text_policy) for discovery,
 tt(grapheme_safe_text) for compiled complete support. The safe path requires
wide ncurses with wide-array drawing and readback, multibyte support,
 tt(nl_langinfo) with tt(CODESET), UTF-8 and tt(MULTIBYTE). Unknown policies, including
 tt(unicode-17.0.0-egc-max-wcwidth-emoji2), are explicitly rejected with status 2.
Invalid input, limits, geometry, assignment and curses errors return 1.
Discovery has no terminal I/O and works before init, while suspended and after end.

The policy name pins boundary data, not libc tables or terminal shaping.
A layout engine must match scalar widths, locale and validation as well as the
profile, and handle per-text native-storage rejection. Compare representative
queries; identical locale names alone do not prove identical width tables.
Padding starts at measured width. Snapshots retain ordinary native cells and
wide continuations, not synthetic two-cell emoji or persistent grapheme records.
Region operations, prepared rows and tt(textwrap) keep their existing cell
contracts. Callers must align overwrite origins, copies, viewport edges and
shrinking resizes using source text and tt(textpos); these operations can split
a previously drawn multi-cell grapheme. Boundaries are protected within one
opted-in call, not across calls. Exact shaped widths or intra-grapheme styles
would require a separate rendering architecture.

The subcommand tt(textwrap) greedily wraps one printable logical line into rows
within a positive literal decimal column budget, at most tt(INT_MAX). Clipping
units and widths follow tt(textinfo): a spacing character keeps its following
zero-width characters. A row ends before the next unit that would exceed the
budget. A unit wider than the budget fails the whole query. Spaces are retained
exactly; words may cross rows. Tabs, newlines and other controls are rejected.
Applications own logical-line splitting, word-breaking and indentation policy.

Empty text returns one empty row. An exact fit does not add a trailing empty row.
Input is limited to 1048576 original encoded bytes and output to 4096 rows.
These bounds limit result storage but do not specify exact peak bytes for
internal escaped strings, temporary storage and hash entries. The ordinary
writable association is replaced or created with:
startsitem()
sitem(tt(format))(tt(zdraw-textwrap-1).)
sitem(tt(columns))(Requested per-row column budget.)
sitem(tt(line_count))(Number of rows, including one for empty text.)
sitem(tt(total_bytes), tt(total_width))(Original byte length and unwrapped width.)
sitem(tt(byte_limit), tt(line_limit))(Compiled input-byte and output-row limits.)
sitem(var(N)tt(,text))(Original text for zero-based row var(N).)
sitem(var(N)tt(,byte_start), var(N)tt(,byte_end))(Half-open source byte range.)
sitem(var(N)tt(,column_start), var(N)tt(,column_end))(Half-open column range in
the unwrapped source.)
sitem(var(N)tt(,width))(Row width, the difference between its column endpoints.)
endsitem()

Byte offsets count original encoded bytes, not shell character indices or
internal escapes. Concatenating rows in order reconstructs the input exactly.
Ranges remain contiguous even when a row leaves unused columns before a wide
unit. To hit-test a row, first validate the local column against that row's width,
then add its tt(column_start) and query the original text with tt(textpos).
Source byte offsets can retain a selection across reflow.

All rows are prepared before assignment. Invalid input, excess bytes or rows,
and invalid arguments leave the destination unchanged or absent. Status is 0 on
success, 1 for those errors or assignment failure, and 2 for non-ASCII input on
a build without tt(wide_text). Check tt(text_wrapping) for the command. It needs
no tt(init) or terminal, reads no input, changes no drawing state and does not
present queued frames. It scans the input once, retaining only row ranges and
returned text with no persistent cache.

These are clipping units, not Unicode grapheme clusters or word boundaries.
The current locale and tt(MULTIBYTE) setting determine widths; terminal shaping
can differ. Analysis does not enforce curses' combining-character storage limit.
Recompute after changing the text, budget or locale.


The subcommand tt(truecolor) accepts exactly tt(on) or tt(off).  It enables or
disables tt(#RRGGBB) color arguments in tt(attr), tt(bg) and tt(spans).
RGB is disabled initially.  Repeating tt(init) preserves the setting;
tt(end) resets it.  Status 0 indicates success, 1 invalid arguments or use
outside a curses session, and 2 that tt(on) is unsupported by the current build,
library or terminal description.  tt(off) succeeds within any session.

The implementation requires optional ncurses extended-color functions,
successful color initialization, exactly 16777216 advertised colors, and an
RGB capability with eight bits per channel: Boolean tt(RGB), numeric tt(RGB#8),
or string tt(RGB=8/8/8).  A read-only library color-content query checks the
encoding.  The compiled tt(truecolor) feature does not imply runtime support;
tt(colorinfo) reports support separately from whether the application opted in.
A large color count, tt(COLORTERM), or palette redefinition support is not enough.
Select a suitable direct-color terminal description before starting the shell;
the module neither changes tt(TERM) nor probes the physical terminal.

When enabled, either side of a color pair can use tt(#) followed by exactly six
hexadecimal digits, in either case.  Quote these arguments.  RGB can be mixed
with existing named or decimal colors and with tt(default) when supported.
RGB values are passed as integers to tt(init_extended_pair), but pair IDs and
decimal indices retain their existing bounds.  Drawing and tt(querychar) use the
same pairs; readback retains the cached spelling.  Existing narrower limits of
tt(bg), tt(spans) and tt(querychar) still apply.  Rejected RGB arguments and
allocation failures return status 1 through these drawing commands.

Some direct-color descriptions reserve low values for ANSI palette indices.
The tt(CO) numeric capability gives the reservation; when absent, eight indices
are conservatively reserved.  RGB values below tt(rgb_min) are rejected.
For example, tt(xterm-direct) normally reserves 0 through 7, so tt(#000000)
is rejected while tt(black) still selects palette black, which need not be exact
RGB black.  A description with tt(CO#0) and matching direct RGB setters supports
the full range.  The module trusts the selected description and does not
approximate unavailable colors or redefine the palette.

Turning RGB off rejects new RGB arguments, including cached pair references,
without changing existing cells, pairs, or window styles.  Existing styles can
still be used by subsequent drawing.  Re-enabling permits cache reuse.  Pairs
are never recycled; different spellings can consume separate pairs and exhaustion
fails normally.  tt(end) clears the cache through the existing session cleanup.

The tt(truecolor) subcommand and capability checks allocate no pairs, read no
terminal input, emit no terminal output, and do not refresh pending drawing or
change input ownership.  There is no additional protocol negotiation or reply
parser.  Curses owns rendering and cleanup, and input remains with tt(input).

The subcommand tt(position) writes various positions associated with
var(targetwin) into the array named var(array).
These are, in order:
startsitem()
sitem(-)(The y and x coordinates of the cursor relative to the top left
of var(targetwin))
sitem(-)(The y and x coordinates of the top left of var(targetwin) on the
screen)
sitem(-)(The size of var(targetwin) in y and x dimensions.)
endsitem()

Outputting characters and strings are achieved by tt(char) and tt(string)
respectively.

The subcommand tt(spans) draws one row of styled text in a single call.
At least one var(style)/var(text) pair is required.  The zero-based var(row) and
var(column) must be unsigned decimal integers within the window.  Each style
is empty or a comma-separated list of attribute names from tt(zdraw_attrs)
and at most one var(foreground)tt(/)var(background) color pair, using the same
color names or decimal indices as tt(attr).  Empty tokens, whitespace in tokens,
and tt(+)/tt(-) attribute changes are rejected.  Arguments are never evaluated.

Each span has a complete style: omitted attributes are off and an omitted color
uses reserved pair 0.  Window attributes, background attributes and background
character substitution do not affect the supplied text.  Explicit
tt(default/default) uses the existing color cache behavior.  Empty text is valid;
its style is checked but allocates no colors.  Drawing preserves the window's
cursor, attributes, color pair and background, does not read input, and becomes
visible only on tt(refresh).  It neither wraps nor scrolls, including at the
bottom-right cell.  Normal curses repair of an overwritten wide character can
clear adjoining cells belonging to that character.

Text must be printable and fit in the remaining columns; it is not clipped.
Controls, tabs, newlines, NULs, invalid encoding and unrepresentable characters
are rejected.  With the tt(wide_spans) feature, text is decoded using the current
locale and the tt(MULTIBYTE) option, and measured with the system character-width
function.  Non-ASCII text requires tt(MULTIBYTE).  Zero-width characters must
follow a spacing character within the same span and fit within curses' complex
character capacity; silent truncation of combining sequences is rejected.
The default span path does not implement Unicode grapheme segmentation or guarantee emoji
sequence rendering.  Without tt(wide_spans), only printable ASCII is supported.

The status is 0 on success, 1 for invalid input, insufficient row space,
allocation failure or a curses error, and 2 when span drawing is unavailable or
non-ASCII text requires an unavailable wide implementation.  The compiled
features tt(styled_spans) and tt(wide_spans) allow callers to select a fallback.
All input is validated before color allocation and drawing.  Pairs share the
existing cache and are not recycled.  A later allocation failure retains earlier
successful allocations but changes no cells.  A curses write error can leave
partial drawing.  The tt(spans_pair_limit) field of tt(colorinfo) describes the
additional pair-ID limit of the ASCII implementation; an unrepresentable pair
is rejected instead of truncated.

The subcommand tt(spansclip) uses the same style/text interface as tt(spans),
with an additional unsigned decimal column budget after the coordinates.
One budget is shared across all spans and capped by the space remaining to the
window's right edge.  Coordinates must be inside the window, even for a zero
budget.  Prefix selection follows the cell-width rules of tt(textinfo).
The original tt(spans) command still rejects overflow instead of clipping.

Each nonempty span must start with a positive-width character.  Keep a base and
its following zero-width characters within the same span, even when adjacent
styles are identical; this avoids assigning conflicting styles within a curses
complex character.  All text and styles are validated, including clipped runs,
before allocating colors or drawing.  Unrepresentable combining sequences are
rejected instead of silently truncated.  Fully omitted or empty spans allocate
no pairs.  A later allocation failure can retain earlier successful allocations
but changes no cells; a curses write error can partially draw.

The tt(clipped_spans) feature describes availability; tt(wide_spans) is required
for non-ASCII drawing.  Styles, cursor and background preservation, status codes,
input ownership and deferred refresh follow tt(spans).  Unused columns and cells
after the prefix remain unchanged, subject to curses' usual repair of an
overwritten wide character; clearing stale text belongs to the application.

The clipping APIs use cell-width rules, not Unicode grapheme segmentation.
Emoji ZWJ sequences, flags and skin-tone sequences can be split between their
positive-width characters.  Zero-width variation selectors remain with the
preceding character but do not change its tt(wcwidth) result.  Ambiguous-width
characters follow the system locale's policy.  Terminal shaping and font policy
may differ from this model.  No Unicode tables, segmentation dependency or
terminal-width probing are added, and grapheme-safe rendering is not promised.

The subcommand tt(prepare) creates an immutable, session-scoped styled row:

example(zdraw prepare heading 'bold,cyan/black' 'CPU ' 'green/black' '23%'
zdraw draw stdscr 0 0 heading
zdraw draw stdscr 1 0 heading 6
zdraw rowinfo heading row
zdraw unprepare heading)

The name must be an identifier without subscripts, in the module's own
namespace. Duplicate names fail without replacing the original row. Preparation
requires an initialized session but does not depend on a window's dimensions.
It copies decoded cells; changing the original shell strings does not change
those cells. Empty rows are valid and still have their styles checked.

Preparation uses the same text and complete-style validation as tt(spans),
including printable characters, the wide/narrow distinction, combining storage
limits and color syntax. Validation precedes color allocation. Nonempty spans
allocate their colors from the session cache, including cells which a later
clipped draw might omit. A failed allocation can leave earlier successful pairs
cached, but creates no named object and changes no window cells. Preparation
performs no drawing or input.

The subcommand tt(draw) takes a window, row, column and prepared name, followed
by an optional nonnegative decimal column budget. Without a budget the entire
row must fit. With a budget it draws the longest prefix fitting both the budget
and the available columns to the right. Base characters and their stored marks
stay together; double-width cells are never split. This is cell-width clipping,
not full Unicode grapheme segmentation. Even a zero budget or empty row requires
coordinates inside the window. Unused cells are left alone, subject to curses'
repair of overwritten wide characters.

Drawing reuses the prepared cells without reparsing or allocating color pairs.
It does not wrap, scroll, consume input or refresh, and preserves the cursor,
window attributes, color pair and background. A curses write failure may leave
partial drawing. Prepared cells require their original tt(LC_CTYPE) locale name
and tt(MULTIBYTE) option setting, even for ASCII rows. Restore those settings or
prepare another row before drawing. Existing prepared RGB cells remain usable
after tt(truecolor off), like existing window styles; preparing new RGB cells
still requires tt(truecolor on).

The subcommand tt(rowinfo) takes a prepared name and an ordinary writable
associative parameter. An absent target is created and an existing association
is replaced. Readonly/special parameters, non-associations and subscripts are
rejected. An unknown row leaves the target unchanged. The fields are:

startsitem()
sitem(tt(width))(Row width in terminal columns.)
sitem(tt(cells))(Number of stored spacing-character groups, not columns.)
sitem(tt(draws))(Successful draws of this row, including empty and zero-budget
calls, saturating at the tt(counter_limit) reported by tt(resourceinfo).)
sitem(tt(bytes))(Accounted storage of this row.)
sitem(tt(session_bytes))(Total accounted storage of all prepared rows.)
sitem(tt(session_limit))(The session storage limit, 16 MiB.)
sitem(tt(locale))(The tt(LC_CTYPE) name used for preparation.)
sitem(tt(multibyte))(The preparation-time tt(MULTIBYTE) setting, 0 or 1.)
endsitem()

Accounting includes row records, names, locale names, cell arrays and widths.
It excludes hash-table/allocator overhead, transient compilation buffers and
shared color pairs. Preparation checks a conservative capacity bound based on
column width before allocating colors. A wide row may therefore be rejected
even when its eventual stored-cell count would fit.

The subcommand tt(unprepare) frees a named row without changing drawn cells or
reclaiming shared colors. tt(end) and module unload release all prepared rows.
Repeated tt(init) in an existing session keeps them. All four operations require
initialization. Status 0 denotes success, 1 invalid arguments, unknown/duplicate
name, locale mismatch, storage/color failure or curses failure, and 2 unavailable
compiled drawing support (including non-ASCII preparation on a narrow build).
Check the tt(prepared_rows) entry in tt(zdraw_features).

The subcommand tt(event) takes a window, an associative parameter and optional
literals tt(mouse) and tt(norefresh), in either order, once each. It assigns one
structured input record. The target
must be an ordinary writable association or an absent parameter; readonly,
special, scalar, indexed-array and subscripted targets are rejected before input
is read or a size change is acknowledged. Successful assignment replaces the
association, removing fields from earlier events. There is no implicit target.

startsitem()
sitem(tt(type))(tt(character), tt(key), tt(resize) or tt(mouse).)
sitem(tt(source))(tt(curses) for decoded input; tt(terminal) for a detected
terminal-size change.)
sitem(tt(text))(One decoded character or raw byte; empty for non-character events.
Control characters and NUL are possible: this field is not necessarily printable.)
sitem(tt(key))(Empty for characters, otherwise the curses key name without
its tt(KEY_) prefix, such as tt(UP), tt(F5), tt(RESIZE) or tt(MOUSE).
An unrecognized key is represented by its decimal code.)
sitem(tt(code))(Numeric character or library-specific curses key value;
tt(unknown) for a synthesized terminal-size event.)
sitem(tt(encoding))(tt(multibyte) for wide curses input, tt(byte) for narrow
input, or tt(none) for a synthesized size event.)
sitem(tt(modifiers))(tt(unknown) except for mouse events, where it is a
space-separated set of tt(SHIFT), tt(CTRL) and tt(ALT), empty when none were reported.)
sitem(tt(rows), tt(columns))(Present only for resize records.)
sitem(tt(id), tt(x), tt(y), tt(z), tt(buttons))(Present only for mouse records.
Coordinates tt(x) and tt(y) are zero-based screen coordinates, not relative to
the input window. tt(id) and tt(z) are library-reported values. tt(buttons) is a
space-separated set such as tt(PRESSED1), tt(RELEASED1) or tt(CLICKED1), and may
be empty for motion.)
endsitem()

Wide input uses the current locale and reports a numeric wide-character value;
narrow input returns raw bytes 0--255. Curses key numbers vary by library.
Character records do not identify unmodified physical keys. Ordinary curses
records do not distinguish press/repeat/release or exact keyboard modifiers.
Focus, enhanced keyboard input, bracketed paste and capability replies have
separate explicitly enabled record formats described below.

The existing tt(input) and new tt(event) share the same curses queue and decoder.
Applications choose which call consumes each item. There is no background reader;
do not read the terminal concurrently through shell tt(read), ZLE or another
process. tt(event) enables keypad decoding and uses the window's tt(timeout).
Zero polls. A finite positive timeout allows applications to observe resizes
without keyboard input. Escape decoding and inherited EINTR retries can extend
the wait; it is not a strict overall deadline. Curses may refresh a modified
window during input, just as with the inherited tt(input) operation. Complete
and present a frame before reading, or use tt(norefresh).

The tt(poll) flag temporarily uses a zero initial timeout and restores the
window's configured timeout on return. It can be combined with tt(mouse) and
tt(norefresh); all flags are distinct and order-independent. Native escape
decoding and EINTR behavior still apply, so this is not a hard nonblocking
deadline. Empty input returns 1 without assigning the destination.

The subcommand tt(capabilities) passively assigns an ordinary writable association
(or absent parameter), including before initialization or while suspended. It
opens no terminal, sends no query, reads no input and changes no enabled state.
The format is tt(zdraw-capabilities-1). Top-level keys are tt(names), tt(session)
(tt(inactive), tt(active), tt(suspended)), tt(term), tt(locale), tt(curses_version)
and tt(query_owner). Terminal names describe configuration, not verified identity.

For each name, comma-separated fields are tt(compiled) (tt(yes) or tt(no)),
tt(support) (tt(yes), tt(no), tt(unknown)), tt(source), tt(evidence_support),
tt(evidence_source), tt(enabled), tt(reported) and tt(query). Evidence sources are
tt(none), tt(compiled), tt(curses), tt(terminfo), tt(reply) or tt(override).
Optional literal var(name)tt(=yes|no|unknown) arguments affect only the returned
support/source fields; original evidence remains separately available. Overrides
are not retained and never bypass native availability checks. Unknown names,
duplicate overrides and invalid values fail before assignment.

Names tt(colors) and tt(truecolor) report the existing session's curses and
terminfo evidence. tt(wide_text), tt(norefresh_events) and tt(suspend_resume)
report compiled API support; wide text does not establish font or emoji coverage.
Names tt(streaming_paste), tt(focus_events) and tt(synchronized_output) report
terminal recognition of modes 2004, 1004 and 2026, independently of compiled API
support. Synchronized-output activation configures tt(present) through tt(sync).
tt(keyboard_events) reports recognition of the kitty keyboard protocol; its
tt(reported) field is the accepted decimal flag value, or tt(unknown).
Before evidence exists, terminal support remains tt(unknown). The tt(enabled)
field describes module-owned persistent activation, not terminal confirmation;
operations with no persistent activation report tt(no).

With an initialized session, tt(query on) reserves 47 exact seven-bit replies
(15 DECRPM and 32 keyboard reports) in the existing curses decoder. It emits nothing and enables no
terminal mode. tt(query request) accepts one of tt(streaming_paste),
tt(focus_events), tt(synchronized_output), tt(keyboard_events), and literal decimal milliseconds from
20 through 5000. It writes one DECRQM or keyboard request and flushes stdout. One request may
be outstanding; requests during an active paste fail. Each mode may be attempted
once per session, even after cancellation or a failed write: replies lack request
identifiers, so a retry could mistake an old reply for a new one.

While ownership is enabled, tt(event) returns tt(type=capability),
tt(source=decrpm), tt(name), numeric tt(mode), tt(phase), numeric tt(report) and
empty tt(text). A tt(reply) phase accepts evidence only for the pending mode
before its monotonic deadline. Report 0 means unrecognized; 1 through 4 mean set,
reset, permanently set and permanently reset. Recognition does not establish
that a permanently reset mode can be enabled. The tt(reported) capability field
records these as tt(unrecognized), tt(set), tt(reset), tt(permanent-set),
tt(permanent-reset), or tt(unknown) without accepted evidence.

Keyboard queries send tt(CSI ? u) and accept current flag values 0 through 31.
Their events use tt(source=kitty-query), tt(mode=0), and numeric tt(report).
Zero confirms protocol recognition with no flags set; it does not mean
unrecognized. Larger future flag combinations are not accepted as evidence.

A tt(timeout) event has report -1 and leaves support unknown. Other recognized
replies are tt(unsolicited) or tt(late) events without changing evidence.
The tt(query) field is tt(never), tt(pending), tt(replied), tt(timeout),
tt(cancelled), tt(send-error), or tt(unavailable) for non-queryable entries.
Inspection can report an expired deadline without consuming its timeout event.
Only event calls process deadlines; use finite external wait ticks. The initial
input wait is capped by the deadline, but curses escape decoding and inherited
signal retries may extend the call. This is an acceptance deadline, not a hard
execution-time bound. Querying requires ncurses key registration and a monotonic
clock; check tt(capability_queries). Passive inspection has tt(capability_evidence).

Curses retains its existing decoding for ordinary keys, malformed/unsupported
reports and fragments that exceed its escape delay. Only exact supported replies
are intercepted; report-shaped data inside paste remains payload. Replies are
input bytes, not authenticated evidence. While queries own decoding, tt(input)
is rejected; use tt(event) and no concurrent shell, ZLE or external reader.

The idempotent tt(query cancel) cancels without a synthetic event or output.
The idempotent tt(query off) cancels and releases registrations, preserving stored
evidence and attempt limits. Neither flushes input; later reports after off may
become ordinary input. Successful tt(suspend) cancels without retry on resume.
Bytes during a handoff belong to the other foreground owner. tt(end) and unload
clear registrations, observations and attempts. Repeated active tt(init) preserves
them. Status is 0 on success, 1 on invalid input/state, collisions or library/I/O
failure, and 2 for queries without compiled support. On systems supporting
terminal process groups, background tt(resume) returns 1 before changing modes;
applications resume after foreground handoff and retain their own signal traps.

The subcommand tt(focus on) requires an accepted mode-1004 tt(reset) report,
then enables reporting and reserves the exact tt(CSI I) and tt(CSI O) sequences.
Requiring reset avoids taking over an already enabled or permanent mode.
tt(focus on force) explicitly assumes ownership without that evidence; neither
form changes recorded evidence. Existing matching terminfo tt(kxIN)/tt(kxOUT)
definitions are borrowed and preserved; other collisions fail without replacement.
Events contain tt(type=focus), tt(source=focus-report), tt(focused=1) or tt(0),
and empty tt(text). Application field focus and selection are unchanged.
tt(focus off) disables reporting and releases newly allocated definitions.

The subcommand tt(keyboard on) requires an accepted keyboard query reply; there
is no force form. It pushes kitty flag value 27, requesting disambiguation,
event types, all-key reporting and associated text, without alternate-layout
reporting. It does not independently confirm each requested enhancement.
tt(keyboard off) pops that entry, restoring the previous flags. Activation during
an active paste fails. Both protocols' on/off calls are idempotent. Check compiled
features tt(focus_events) and tt(keyboard_events); terminal support is separate.

Enhanced keyboard records contain tt(type=key), tt(source=kitty), and:
startsitem()
sitem(tt(key), tt(code), tt(code_kind))(A tt(U+) codepoint identity, supported
functional name or tt(UNKNOWN); the decimal protocol number; and tt(unicode)
or tt(functional). Functional CSI numbers need tt(key)/tt(raw) to distinguish
encodings. CSI-u Escape, Enter, Tab, Backspace and F13 through F35 have names.
CSI letter/tilde forms support arrows, Home/End, Insert/Delete, Page Up/Down,
F1 through F12 and Menu. Other protocol private-use identities are unsupported.)
sitem(tt(supported))(tt(yes) for known identities, otherwise tt(no) with the
numeric identity preserved.)
sitem(tt(action))(tt(press), tt(repeat) or tt(release); an omitted wire event
type defaults to press.)
sitem(tt(modifiers), tt(modifier_bits))(Space-separated tt(SHIFT ALT CTRL SUPER
HYPER META CAPS_LOCK NUM_LOCK) in that order, empty for none; and the decoded
0 through 255 bitmask.)
sitem(tt(text), tt(encoding), tt(text_status))(Only explicitly associated text,
encoded as UTF-8 even with narrow curses input; tt(encoding=utf-8) and
tt(text_status=provided) or tt(none). At most 16 Unicode scalars (64 bytes),
including possible NUL/control characters. Never infer text from key identity or
Shift. Applications should ignore release events for text insertion.)
sitem(tt(shifted_key), tt(base_key))(tt(unknown). Alternate fields are bounded
and validated but not exposed as physical-layout claims.)
sitem(tt(raw))(Original bounded wire bytes, safely stored in the shell string
but unsafe to print directly to a terminal.)
endsitem()

Curses handles existing keys, mouse, focus, paste starts and query replies first.
Only an otherwise literal Escape followed immediately on the same queue by an
opening square bracket enters the enhanced CSI-tail parser. Standalone Escape
and non-CSI prefixes retain legacy decoding. Ordinary curses records retain
their existing fields; no repeat/release or modifier information is invented.
Paste payload is never interpreted as enhanced input.

The parser retains at most 256 bytes and reads at most 256 bytes per call, with
a 250 ms monotonic tail deadline. Partial state survives empty reads; continuation
waits are capped by the deadline. Initial curses escape timing and signal handling
still affect call duration, and gaps before prefix recognition may leave input
on the legacy path. Numeric fields are bounded to seven digits and Unicode
scalar limits; surrogates, invalid modifiers/actions and excess fields fail.
Unknown or malformed packets produce tt(type=unknown), tt(source=kitty),
tt(supported=no), tt(reason), bounded tt(raw), empty tt(text), tt(text_status=none),
tt(encoding=byte), and unknown key/code/action/modifiers. Oversized packets are
discarded in bounded unknown chunks until their final byte or timeout, keeping
their tail out of text input. A new Escape interrupts a partial packet and is
returned to the queue for normal decoding. Timeout releases partial state;
later bytes cannot reliably be correlated with it.

While either protocol owns decoding, tt(input) is rejected; use tt(event) as the
single terminal input owner. Neither adds a background reader or installs traps.
tt(keyboard off) and tt(suspend) reject partial/discard state: drain it through
tt(event), including its timeout, or end the session. Suspend disables focus and
pops keyboard flags; resume restores configured enhancements. End/unload disable
owned modes and clear the held parser buffer, including incomplete packets, but
cannot retract bytes in transit. Late reports after off may become ordinary input.
Cleanup never re-enables another protocol after a disable failure. A failed
keyboard pop is not retried because a failed flush may have reached the peer;
terminal output failures can leave peer state uncertain. Activation returns 0
on success, 1 for invalid arguments/state or library/I/O failure, and 2 without
compiled support or sufficient evidence. Delivering an unknown event is success;
an incomplete or empty read returns 1 without replacing the output association.

The subcommand tt(inputinfo) requires a session and replaces an ordinary writable
association with input state, without reading or presenting:
startsitem()
sitem(tt(fd))(0 for terminal stdin used by tt(initscr), otherwise -1.)
sitem(tt(queued))(tt(unknown): descriptor readiness cannot reveal the curses queue.)
sitem(tt(wait_ms))(Suggested maximum external wait tick, 20 milliseconds.)
sitem(tt(escape_delay_ms))(Current ncurses escape delay, or tt(unknown).)
sitem(tt(suspended))(Whether this session explicitly released the terminal.)
sitem(tt(paste_enabled), tt(paste_active))(Configured paste ownership and active payload state.)
sitem(tt(paste_pending))(Held possible end-delimiter bytes.)
endsitem()
These fields describe module configuration, not terminal protocol confirmation.
Status is 0 on assignment or 1 for invalid target/state. Check tt(input_info).

The subcommand tt(inputdelay) accepts literal decimal milliseconds from 0 to 1000,
changing ncurses escape-decoder timing explicitly. The first change saves the old
value; tt(end) and unload restore it. Lower values reduce latency but tolerate less
delay between fragments of keys and paste-start delimiters. Check tt(input_delay)
for tt(get_escdelay) and tt(set_escdelay) support. Status is 0 on success, 1 for
invalid arguments/library failure, and 2 without support. Initialization is required.

For asynchronous applications, drain a bounded batch of tt(event) with tt(poll)
and tt(norefresh) before waiting on terminal and worker descriptors with tt(zselect).
Do not read the terminal outside curses. A 20-millisecond external tick is
tt(zselect -t 2), since tt(zselect) uses hundredths of a second. Poll again after
timeouts as well as descriptor readiness: curses may hold buffered input and
terminal geometry can change without input bytes. Remove worker descriptors at
EOF and bound event batches to avoid starving worker output. No extra reader or
event-loop framework is provided. Check tt(event_poll) for the polling flag.

The subcommand tt(paste on) explicitly requests xterm bracketed paste mode 2004.
It requires terminal stdin/stdout and ncurses tt(define_key), tt(key_defined) and
tt(keybound); check tt(streaming_paste). An unused key code is reserved for the
start delimiter; an already-bound delimiter is rejected. Curses recognizes the
start with its normal escape timing. Once active, tt(event) reads raw payload
bytes from the same queue with keypad decoding disabled, until the end delimiter.
The inherited tt(input) command is rejected while paste owns input.
Paste enablement also owns raw terminal input mode so signal/flow-control bytes
and carriage returns reach the decoder intact. This applies between pastes too:
Ctrl-C is an input character, not a terminal-generated signal, and applications
must handle it. Disabling paste restores pre-enable input modes; suspension
restores shell modes and resume restores the enabled input policy. Paste defaults
to off.

Paste associations contain tt(type=paste), tt(source=bracketed-paste),
tt(encoding=byte), tt(phase=begin), tt(data) or tt(end), raw tt(text), and tt(bytes).
The begin record is empty. Consume text from both data and end records; the end
record can be empty. Each payload record contains at most 4096 original bytes.
UTF-8, NUL, newlines and controls can cross record boundaries. Applications own
decoding, sanitizing, accumulation and total-size limits. Paste text is never
evaluated by the module and must not be treated as shell code or raw terminal output.

The first payload byte uses the requested timeout; subsequent reads poll. Work
per call is bounded. At most five possible end-delimiter bytes survive between
calls, including across arbitrary timeouts; false prefixes are returned as data.
Start-delimiter fragments must arrive within curses' escape timing window.
No automatic paste deadline is imposed. A literal end delimiter terminates paste.
With no record available, status 1 leaves the destination unchanged while retaining
decoder state. Invalid targets and flags are rejected before consuming input.

Repeated tt(paste on) and tt(paste off) are idempotent. tt(off) and tt(suspend)
reject an active paste. tt(end) and unload disable mode 2004, remove the owned key
definition, clear parser state and flush currently queued input when abandoning
an active paste. They cannot retract bytes arriving later. Mode changes emit
control sequences immediately; tt(norefresh) still prevents frame presentation.
Status is 0 on success, 1 for invalid state/arguments or library/output failure,
and 2 without compiled support. Initialization is required.

The subcommand tt(suspend) saves program modes, disables configured paste, focus,
keyboard enhancements and active mouse reporting, calls tt(endwin), and restores the modes saved before
initialization. Windows, pads, prepared rows and colors remain allocated.
Repeated suspension is idempotent; active paste and partial keyboard packets must finish first. While suspended,
drawing, input and tt(init) are rejected. tt(resume), tt(suspend), tt(end), tt(resourceinfo),
tt(inputinfo), tt(capabilities), geometry/color queries and headless text queries remain available.
Ending a suspended session frees resources without re-entering the alternate screen.

The subcommand tt(resume) restores program modes, rechecks terminal dimensions,
updates them when tt(resize_term) is available, and repaints the retained virtual
frame. Configured paste, focus, keyboard and mouse reporting are restored. Repeated resume is
idempotent. This is an explicit presentation boundary, including drawing queued
before suspension. Applications still own relayout and viewport recomposition;
changed terminal geometry is reported by the next event where supported. Handoff
does not normally flush input. Shell programs can use an tt(always) block to resume
after a foreground command. No automatic signal or job-control traps are installed.

Check tt(suspend_resume) for tt(def_prog_mode) and tt(reset_prog_mode). Both
commands require a session. Status is 0 on success, 1 for state/library failure,
and 2 without support. Recoverable preparation failures keep the session active;
failed resume mode/resize/repaint leaves it suspended for retry or tt(end).
Terminal-output failure may leave partially applied terminal state.

With tt(norefresh), input uses a private one-cell ncurses pad. The call does not
refresh drawing windows or present pending drawing. It preserves the selected
window's cursor and dirty state, sharing the same input queue, decoder and
window timeout. Use tt(refresh) to present a completed frame explicitly.
Keypad/mouse setup can still emit control sequences; this is not a guarantee of
zero terminal output. The pad is created lazily, is absent from tt(zdraw_windows)
and is released by tt(end) or module unload. No additional protocol is enabled.
This path is currently available only with ncurses; check tt(norefresh_events)
in tt(zdraw_features). Other curses builds return status 2 before reading input
or acknowledging a resize.

When supported, the controlling terminal's dimensions are checked before a
read and after a failed read. A change from the last acknowledged size returns
a tt(source=terminal) resize record with tt(key=RESIZE) and tt(code=unknown).
It consumes no input, changes no windows and does not refresh. The starting
comparison dimensions are those at initialization. Changes between observations
can coalesce, and zero sizes or failed queries are ignored. A decoded curses
resize key instead reports tt(source=curses) and curses' current dimensions.
Applications may receive both kinds and should handle repeated sizes safely.
No signal handler is installed; an indefinite read need not wake on a resize.

The optional tt(mouse) flag requests curses' existing mouse mask for that read.
Supply it on each read that wants reporting. A curses read without it disables
mouse reporting; a synthetic size record does not change input modes.
The tt(mouse) subcommand configures the mask as before. Mouse enable/mask flags
use distinct bits and reset at tt(end), so reporting can be disabled and then
re-enabled in a subsequent session. Applications own hit-testing and shortcuts.

Status 0 means a record was assigned; status 1 covers invalid arguments,
failed reads (including timeout/interruption), missing mouse data or assignment
failure, or input-pad allocation failure. Status 2 means a requested flag
(tt(mouse) or tt(norefresh)) lacks compiled support.
Failed reads leave the target unchanged and do not create an absent target.
Assignment failure after a successful read cannot restore consumed input.
Check tt(structured_events), tt(wide_events) and tt(resize_events) in
 tt(zdraw_features); compiled resize support does not guarantee runtime delivery.

To draw a border around window var(targetwin), use tt(border).  Note
that the border is not subsequently handled specially:  in other words,
the border is simply a set of characters output at the edge of the
window.  Hence it can be overwritten, can scroll off the window, etc.

With no character arguments, tt(border) draws the standard curses border.
Alternatively, supply all eight characters in the order shown above.  An empty
argument selects the corresponding curses default; a space selects a space
instead of a border glyph, subject to curses' normal background-character
substitution.
Each nonempty argument must be a single printable character occupying one
terminal column according to the current locale's system character width.
Control characters, combining marks, multiple characters and double-width
characters are rejected.  Custom borders require at least two rows and columns.
The current cursor position, window attributes and interior cells are preserved;
the border becomes visible on the next tt(refresh).

The tt(custom_borders) entry in tt(zdraw_features) identifies this extended
form.  Printable ASCII characters are always supported.  Other characters
require the tt(wide_borders) compiled feature, a suitable locale and the
tt(MULTIBYTE) option.  The return status is 0 on success, 1 on invalid arguments
or a curses error, and 2 when a non-ASCII character is requested on a build
without wide border support.  Argument validation happens before drawing;
a curses error during drawing does not guarantee that the window is unchanged.

The subcommand tt(attr) will set var(targetwin)'s attributes or
foreground/background color pair for any successive character output.
Each var(attribute) given on the line may be prepended by a tt(+) to set
or a tt(-) to unset that attribute; tt(+) is assumed if absent.  The
attributes supported are tt(blink), tt(bold), tt(dim), tt(reverse),
tt(standout), and tt(underline).

Each var(fg_col)tt(/)var(bg_col) attribute (to be read as
`var(fg_col) on var(bg_col)') sets the foreground and background color
for character output.  The color tt(default) is sometimes available
(in particular if the library is ncurses), specifying the foreground
or background color with which the terminal started.  The color pair
tt(default/default) is always available. To use more than the 8 named
colors (red, green, etc.) construct the var(fg_col)tt(/)var(bg_col)
pairs where var(fg_col) and var(bg_col) are decimal integers, e.g
tt(128/200).  Numeric colors must consist entirely of decimal digits and must
be less than tt(ZDRAW_COLORS) and fit in the C type tt(short).  For example,
indices 0 through 255 are available when the terminal supports 256 colors.
Pair allocation also respects the range of tt(short), even if the library
advertises more pairs.  Exhaustion fails without recycling existing pairs;
already allocated pairs remain usable.  Pairs are cached by their spelling,
so equivalent named and numeric specifications can use separate entries.

tt(bg) overrides the color and other attributes of all characters in the
window.  Its usual use is to set the background initially, but it will
overwrite the attributes of any characters at the time when it is called.
In addition to the arguments allowed with tt(attr), an argument tt(@)var(char)
specifies a character to be shown in otherwise blank areas of the window.
Owing to limitations of curses this cannot be a multibyte character
(use of ASCII characters only is recommended).  As the specified set
of attributes override the existing background, turning attributes
off in the arguments is not useful, though this does not cause an error.

The subcommand tt(scroll) can be used with tt(on) or tt(off) to enabled
or disable scrolling of a window when the cursor would otherwise move
below the window due to typing or output.  It can also be used with a
positive or negative integer to scroll the window up or down the given
number of lines without changing the current cursor position (which
therefore appears to move in the opposite direction relative to the
window).  In the second case, if scrolling is tt(off) it is temporarily
turned tt(on) to allow the window to be scrolled.

The subcommand tt(input) reads a single character from the window
without echoing it back.  If var(param) is supplied the character is
assigned to the parameter var(param), else it is assigned to the
parameter tt(REPLY).

If both var(param) and var(kparam) are supplied, the key is read in
`keypad' mode.  In this mode special keys such as function keys and
arrow keys return the name of the key in the parameter var(kparam).  The
key names are the macros defined in the tt(curses.h) or tt(ncurses.h)
with the prefix `tt(KEY_)' removed; see also the description of the
parameter tt(zdraw_keycodes) below.  Other keys cause a value to be
set in var(param) as before.  On a successful return only one of
var(param) or var(kparam) contains a non-empty string; the other is set
to an empty string.

If var(mparam) is also supplied, tt(input) attempts to handle mouse
input.  This is only available with the ncurses library; mouse handling
can be detected by checking for the exit status of `tt(zdraw mouse)' with
no arguments.  If a mouse
button is clicked (or double- or triple-clicked, or pressed or released with
a configurable delay from being clicked) then var(kparam) is set to the string
tt(MOUSE), and var(mparam) is set to an array consisting of the
following elements:
startitem()
sitem(-)(An identifier to discriminate different input devices; this
is only rarely useful.)
sitem(-)(The x, y and z coordinates of the mouse click relative to
the full screen, as three elements in that order (i.e. the y coordinate
is, unusually, after the x coordinate).  The z coordinate is only
available for a few unusual input devices and is otherwise set to zero.)
sitem(-)(Any events that occurred as separate items; usually
there will be just one.  An event consists of tt(PRESSED), tt(RELEASED),
tt(CLICKED), tt(DOUBLE_CLICKED) or tt(TRIPLE_CLICKED) followed
immediately (in the same element) by the number of the button.)
sitem(-)(If the shift key was pressed, the string tt(SHIFT).)
sitem(-)(If the control key was pressed, the string tt(CTRL).)
sitem(-)(If the alt key was pressed, the string tt(ALT).)
endsitem()

Not all mouse events may be passed through to the terminal window;
most terminal emulators handle some mouse events themselves.  Note
that the ncurses manual implies that using input both with and
without mouse handling may cause the mouse cursor to appear and
disappear.

The subcommand tt(mouse) can be used to configure the use of the mouse.
There is no window argument; mouse options are global.
`tt(zdraw mouse)' with no arguments returns status 0 if mouse handling
is possible, else status 1.  Otherwise, the possible arguments (which
may be combined on the same command line) are as follows.
tt(delay) var(num) takes a nonnegative decimal integer and sets the maximum delay in milliseconds between press and
release events to be considered as a click; the value 0 disables click
resolution, and the default is one sixth of a second.  tt(motion) proceeded
by an optional `tt(PLUS())' (the default) or tt(-) turns on or off
reporting of mouse motion in addition to clicks, presses and releases,
which are always reported. Delivery of motion events depends on the terminal
and curses implementation. For application-owned dragging, use tt(delay 0 motion)
and request mouse input on every read. Mouse options belong to the application;
companions must not silently change another component's configuration.

The subcommand tt(timeout) specifies a timeout value for input from
var(targetwin).  If var(intval) is negative, `tt(zdraw input)' waits
indefinitely for a character to be typed; this is the default.  If
var(intval) is zero, `tt(zdraw input)' returns immediately; if there
is typeahead it is returned, else no input is done and status 1 is
returned.  If var(intval) is positive, `tt(zdraw input)' waits
var(intval) milliseconds for input and if there is none at the end of
that period returns status 1.

The subcommand tt(copy) copies an opaque rectangle of retained cells from
var(sourcewin) to var(targetwin). Source and destination coordinates are
zero-based, nonnegative decimal integers; dimensions are positive decimal
integers. Both rectangles must fit completely. No automatic clipping, resizing
or coordinate evaluation occurs. The area is limited to 65536 occupied columns
summed across all rows per call.

The source rectangle is staged in a private pad before any destination write.
Overlapping copies use the original source cells, including copies between
parent/subwindows that share storage. The pad is released before returning.
Spaces overwrite destination cells. Stored attributes, color pairs and combining
marks are copied without text conversion, background substitution or pair
allocation; existing RGB pairs remain usable after tt(truecolor off).
Cursors, current attributes and backgrounds are preserved. The command does
not wrap, scroll, read input or refresh. Shared subwindow contents change as
usual; tt(touch) another view before refreshing it if its curses change markers
need synchronization.

Opaque curses tt(copywin) calls copy occupied columns, not reconstructed
character layouts. Align both source and destination horizontal edges to
complete characters. Edges splitting wide characters inherit the linked
library's behavior and can leave partial retained glyphs; portable repair and
terminal appearance are not guaranteed. This includes subwindow edges that
split parent glyphs. The original text and tt(textpos) can guide alignment.

Check tt(region_copy) for compiled tt(copywin) and tt(newpad) support. Status is
0 on successful copying and pad cleanup, 1 on invalid arguments, resource limits
or library failure, and 2 when compiled support is unavailable. Validation,
allocation and source-staging failures leave the destination untouched. A
failed destination write can leave a partial copy; there is no rollback.
A cleanup failure returns 1 even if the destination copy completed.

The subcommand tt(overlay) takes the same arguments and 65536-cell budget as
tt(copy), but preserves destination cells where the source contains a plain
space: no attributes, color pair zero, and no combining suffix. Styled or colored
spaces remain opaque, as do non-space background characters. Classification uses
stored values, not visual color equality; there is no alpha blending or background
substitution. tt(copy) remains fully opaque.

The source is snapshotted before writes, including aliased/overlapping copies.
Opaque runs use native tt(copywin), preserving cursor/current-style/background
state and allocating no pairs. Wide readers inspect complete stored characters;
narrow readers compare the packed native cell with an unstyled space and do not
reconstruct wide Unicode data. Align source/destination/subwindow edges to whole
glyphs: split-wide edges inherit curses behavior without portable repair.
No input or presentation occurs. Bounds/allocation/source-copy failures leave the
target intact; later inspection or run-write failures may leave partial output,
with no rollback. Cleanup failure returns 1 even if writes completed. Check
tt(transparent_copy) for tt(copywin) and tt(newpad); statuses match tt(copy).

The subcommand tt(restyle) replaces the stored colors and attributes of a
rectangle without replacing its character data, spaces or combining marks.
It takes a complete tt(spans)-style specification: comma-separated attributes
and at most one foreground/background pair. An omitted color uses pair zero;
an empty style clears all attributes and uses pair zero. This does not toggle
attributes or remember earlier styles. The application owns any restoration
style; tt(+bold) and tt(-bold) are invalid.

Coordinates are zero-based, nonnegative decimal integers and dimensions are
positive decimal integers. The whole rectangle must fit. Geometry and style
validation precede color allocation or cell changes. RGB arguments require
the existing tt(truecolor) opt-in, including existing pair-cache entries.

One curses tt(wchgat) call is made per row. No text conversion, cell buffer,
wrapping, scrolling or refresh occurs. Existing Unicode cells can be restyled
independently of the caller's text decoding locale. Current drawing attributes
and background remain unchanged. The live cursor is restored when library calls
permit it. Shared subwindow cells change normally; tt(touch) other views when
their change markers need synchronization before refresh.

Align horizontal edges to complete characters in existing wide text. The linked
library's occupied-column behavior applies; there is no partial-glyph repair
or portable appearance guarantee. Complete replacement clears attributes outside
the supported style vocabulary, including the alternate-character-set marker
on legacy ACS borders. Their stored codes remain but their displayed meaning
can change. Redraw such borders or use explicit Unicode characters as needed.

Check tt(region_restyle) for compiled tt(wchgat) support; array writers and text
readers are not required. Status is 0 if all rows and cursor restoration succeed,
1 for invalid arguments, color allocation, movement or update failures, and 2
if compiled support is unavailable. Failed updates can leave earlier rows or
part of the failing row changed. Allocated pairs remain cached. Cursor restoration
is attempted after an update failure; if restoration itself fails, the cursor
may remain at the last operation's position. There is no rollback. Older ncurses
ABIs that pack pair IDs reject values outside their packed representation.

The subcommand tt(fill) fills a rectangle by repeating one styled single-column
cell. Arguments are the window, zero-based row and column, positive row and
column counts, style, and tile text. Coordinates and dimensions are literal
decimal integers. Zero dimensions and rectangles extending outside the window
are rejected; there is no clipping or implicit expansion.

A tile is one positive-width character occupying exactly one column, optionally
followed by combining marks on a wide drawing build. Empty text, multiple spacing
characters, two-column characters, leading combining marks and controls fail.
Style syntax, color allocation and storage limits are shared with tt(spans).
Printable ASCII works with the narrow writer; non-ASCII requires tt(wide_spans).
RGB styles require truecolor opt-in. Geometry and the whole tile/style are
validated before drawing. Invalid geometry, text or styles allocate no colors
and change no cells.

The tile is compiled once and one repeated row is reused for all writes. Cursor,
current attributes and background are preserved; no wrapping, scrolling or
refresh occurs. A space tile remains a literal space regardless of background.
The bottom-right cell can be filled. No prepared object is retained.

Overwriting part of an existing wide character follows the linked curses array
writer, as with tt(spans). Repairs may affect the character's occupied cells
outside the rectangle. Align boundaries to complete characters when surrounding
wide text must be preserved; tt(textpos) can find those boundaries in source text.
The rectangle is not a strict mutation boundary for intersecting wide characters.

Check tt(region_fill) for compiled support. Status is 0 if all writes succeed,
1 on invalid arguments, color allocation or library write failure, and 2 if the
writer is unavailable or a non-ASCII tile needs wide support. A failed write may
leave previous rows and part of the failing row changed; drawing is not rolled
back. The shared writer restores cursor/style state where library calls permit.

The subcommand tt(cellinfo) reads the retained cell at a window's current
cursor into an ordinary writable associative parameter, creating it if absent
and replacing it if present. It requires an initialized session. It does not
move the cursor, touch or refresh the window, change drawing style, consume
input or allocate colors. Unpresented drawing is visible to the query.
The association has these fields:
startsitem()
sitem(tt(text))(Complete stored wide-character text, including combining marks,
or the library's byte cell representation with narrow readback.)
sitem(tt(characters))(Stored wide-character count, or 1 for byte readback.)
sitem(tt(encoding))(tt(multibyte) or tt(byte), identifying the readback path.)
sitem(tt(row), tt(column))(Zero-based cursor position relative to the window.)
sitem(tt(attributes))(Space-separated known flags: tt(blink), tt(bold), tt(dim),
 tt(reverse), tt(standout), tt(underline), and tt(altcharset) when present.)
sitem(tt(attribute_bits))(Decimal mask of all non-color attribute bits, including
unnamed flags; this value is library-specific.)
sitem(tt(color))(Original color-pair spelling in the module cache, or tt(unknown).)
sitem(tt(color_source))(tt(cache) or tt(unknown), identifying the evidence.)
sitem(tt(pair))(Curses color-pair ID; this number is session-specific.)
endsitem()

Check tt(cell_inspection) and tt(wide_cell_inspection) for compiled support.
Wide readback converts the entire stored text using the current locale,
independently of the tt(MULTIBYTE) option. If that locale cannot represent the
text, the query fails. It cannot recover characters discarded by curses storage
limits. Narrow readback cannot reconstruct wide text and has the same packed
pair limit as tt(querychar), reported by tt(colorinfo)'s tt(query_pair_limit).
Colors above that limit cannot be inspected reliably. Alternate-character-set
values can render differently from their tt(text) representation.

Colors are reported from the cache, not from the terminal's palette. Named and
numeric spellings remain distinct, and existing RGB spellings remain readable
after truecolor opt-out. Uncached pairs report tt(unknown). Neither tt(pair) nor
 tt(attribute_bits) is a portable cross-session comparison value.

On ncurses, reading a continuation column of a wide character returns the same
stored text while retaining the requested cursor location in tt(row) and
 tt(column). This query does not identify continuation cells or the leading
column and is not a complete screen serialization format.

Return status is 0 on assignment, or 1 on invalid arguments, failed library
read, conversion or assignment. Readonly/special parameters, indexed arrays,
scalars and subscripts are rejected before reading. Failures leave the target
unchanged and do not create a missing parameter. The inherited tt(querychar)
interface remains unchanged.

The subcommand tt(snapshot) captures a whole retained window into an ordinary
writable association, replacing it or creating it if absent. It requires an
initialized session. The query reads an independent temporary copy and does not
move the live cursor, touch or refresh the window, change its drawing styles,
or consume input. Subwindows use their own relative coordinates. The copy is
released before assignment, also on read or conversion failure. Result strings
belong to the shell and survive drawing, window deletion, tt(end) and unload.

Metadata keys are tt(format) with value tt(zdraw-snapshot-1), tt(layout) with
value tt(readback), tt(rows), tt(columns), tt(cursor_row), tt(cursor_column),
 tt(cell_count), tt(cell_limit) and tt(byte_limit). Dimensions and cursor values
refer to the captured window. Limits are 65536 cells and 16 MiB of accounted
key/value bytes. The latter includes metadata, internal string escaping and NUL
terminators, but excludes allocator, association-node and temporary-window overhead.
Cell count is checked before duplicating the window. Budget failures return no
partial result.

Each coordinate has all ten tt(cellinfo) fields under keys of the form
 tt(row,column,field), with zero-based decimal coordinates. For example,
 tt(frame[2,5,text]) is the cell text at row 2, column 5. Encoding and color
limitations are identical to tt(cellinfo). On ncurses, wide-character text is
repeated at occupied continuation columns; by default no leading-cell or grapheme
metadata is inferred. Concatenating cell text is not a way to reconstruct a rendered row.

The optional final argument tt(occupancy), advertised by tt(cell_occupancy),
adds tt(occupancy), tt(occupancy_source), tt(base_column), tt(cell_width) and
 tt(style_supported) to every coordinate. Printable single-column cells report
 tt(single), tt(readback) and their own base column. Wide equal-text/style/pair
runs bounded on both sides by different cells and divisible by the system width
report tt(base) or tt(continuation), with source tt(inferred). This is conservative
inference from public readback, not access to authoritative library flags.
Runs touching either horizontal edge, clipped/shared edges and invalid or partial
runs report tt(unknown) for occupancy, source and base. Width is the first stored
character's positive system width, or tt(unknown). Corrupted wide storage may be
indistinguishable from complete runs; this metadata does not prove rendering.
The tt(style_supported) field is tt(yes) if every non-color attribute bit has a
supported style name, otherwise tt(no), including alternate-character-set flags.
Default snapshots retain their original fields. The same cell and byte limits
apply to the optional fields. No grapheme segmentation is performed.

The versioned record is for inspection and comparison, not terminal-image capture,
restoration or a binary curses-window dump. Compare format, dimensions and the
fields of interest. Pair IDs and raw attribute bits remain library/session
specific, and cached color spellings are not normalized identities. Association
iteration order is unspecified. Quote stored values when printing diagnostics.

Check tt(window_snapshots) for compiled support. Status is 0 on assignment and
1 on invalid arguments, failed copy/read/conversion, exceeded limits or failed
assignment. Invalid targets are rejected before copying. Failures before
assignment preserve an existing result and do not create an absent target. The module retains no
persistent snapshot object.

The subcommand tt(querychar) queries the character at the current cursor
position.  The return values are stored in the array named var(param) if
supplied, else in the array tt(reply).  The first value is the character
(which may be a multibyte character if the system supports them).  Only the
first character is returned for a cell with combining marks.  The second
value is the color pair in the usual var(fg_col)tt(/)var(bg_col)
notation, or tt(0) if color is not supported.  Any attributes other than
color that apply to the character, as set with the subcommand tt(attr),
appear as additional elements.

The subcommand tt(resize) resizes tt(stdscr) and all windows to given
dimensions (windows that stick out from the new dimensions are resized
down). The underlying curses extension (tt(resize_term call)) can be
unavailable. To verify, zeroes can be used for var(height) and
var(width). If the result of the subcommand is tt(0), resize_term is
available (tt(2) otherwise). Tests show that resizing can be normally
accomplished by calling tt(zdraw end) and tt(zdraw refresh). The
tt(resize) subcommand is provided for versatility. Multiple system
configurations have been checked and tt(zdraw end) and tt(zdraw
refresh) are still needed for correct terminal state after resize. To
invoke them with tt(resize), use var(endwin) argument.  Using
var(nosave) argument will cause new terminal state to not be saved
internally by tt(zdraw). This is also provided for versatility and
should normally be not needed.
)
enditem()

subsect(Experimental raster surfaces)

The tt(raster) subcommand is a bounded R1/R2 experiment, not a supported graphics
API. Its interface may change or be removed. Loading the module does not allocate
raster resources. Except for tt(blit), its operations work without tt(init), a
terminal, output or input reads. All raster operations are rejected during a
suspended terminal session. The operations are:

startsitem()
sitem(tt(create) var(name) var(width) var(height) var(color) var(ascii) [ var(color) var(ascii) ... ])(
Create a named surface with a palette of 1 to 32 entries. Material IDs start at
zero. Each color is a decimal terminal index in 0 to 255; each fallback character
is one printable ASCII byte. Names are identifiers of at most 64 bytes, without
subscripts. Duplicate names fail. Dimensions are 1 to 512, with at most 65536
pixels per surface. Pixels initially contain material zero and inverse depth zero.)
sitem(tt(clear) var(name) var(material) [ var(lower_material) ])(
Reset depths to zero and fill materials. The optional second material applies to
rows beginning at height divided by two, rounded down.)
sitem(tt(resize) var(name) var(width) var(height))(
Replace the buffers with cleared buffers, preserving the palette. Failure leaves
the old surface intact.)
sitem(tt(triangles) var(name) [ var(x0) var(y0) var(q0) var(x1) var(y1) var(q1) var(x2) var(y2) var(q2) var(material) ... ])(
Submit already near-clipped screen-space triangles. Coordinates are bounded by
32768 in magnitude; positive inverse depths are at most 1000000. Numbers are
literal decimal values, optionally signed or with an exponent, never arithmetic
expressions. Non-finite values and invalid material IDs are rejected. A call
accepts at most 4096 triangles and 16777216 clipped bounding-box pixel visits;
the whole batch is validated before pixels change. Both windings are accepted.
Sampling uses pixel centers and inclusive barycentric weights of at least
-0.000001; absolute double areas at most 0.000001 are degenerate. Inverse depth
is affine in screen space. Only a strictly greater computed depth replaces the
previous material. Equal computed depths keep the earlier pixel.)
sitem(tt(read) var(name) var(array))(
Replace an ordinary writable indexed array with row-major alternating material
and depth values. This diagnostic path is not intended for frame presentation.)
sitem(tt(info) var(name) var(association))(
Report dimensions, palette size, byte accounting and limits. The format tag is
tt(zdraw-raster-experiment-1). Up to 32 live surfaces and 8 MiB of owned raster
storage are permitted. Transient old and new resize buffers must fit that budget.
Bounded per-call scratch, shell parameters and curses storage are additional.)
sitem(tt(blit) var(name) var(window) var(row) var(column) tt(half)|tt(ascii)|tt(mono))(
Pack into width by ceiling of height divided by two cells; the complete rectangle
must fit. Half blocks use upper foreground and lower background; a missing lower
pixel uses material zero. ASCII mode uses the upper pixel's glyph and color with
material zero's background. Mono uses the upper glyph and pair zero. Half mode
requires wide-cell support and a width-one half block in the current multibyte
locale. Unavailable support or palette colors return status 2. Invalid arguments,
resource limits, pair exhaustion and drawing failures return 1. The complete
rectangle is compiled before window writes; a later curses write failure can
partially change retained cells. Cursor, background and attributes are preserved.
No input is read or presentation performed. Use tt(stage) or tt(viewport), then
tt(present). Pairs use the immutable session cache and are never recycled while
retained cells could refer to them.)
sitem(tt(free) var(name))(
Release the surface without changing previously drawn cells. tt(end) and module
unload release all raster surfaces, including surfaces created headlessly.)
endsitem()

The tt(resourceinfo) result additionally includes tt(raster_surfaces),
tt(raster_bytes) and tt(raster_byte_limit). Full experimental contracts,
reference fixtures and measurement instructions accompany the module in
tt(docs/raster-experiment.md).

subsect(Parameters)

startitem()
vindex(zdraw_features)
item(tt(zdraw_features))(
Readonly array of optional features compiled into tt(zdraw), available
as soon as the parameter is enabled when loading the module.  Reading it does
not initialise curses, access the terminal, consume input, or produce output.
Its contents are unchanged by tt(init), tt(end), or terminal availability.

The following feature names are defined:
startsitem()
sitem(tt(window_movement))(Move independent ordinary windows.)
sitem(tt(window_resize))(Resize and optionally reposition independent ordinary windows.)
sitem(tt(window_trees))(Bounded shared-tree geometry reconstruction.)
sitem(tt(offscreen_pads))(Bounded offscreen pads and viewport staging.)
sitem(tt(pad_resize))(Resize public pads while retaining overlap and drawing state.)
sitem(tt(staged_refresh))(Stage ordinary windows and explicitly present composed frames.)
sitem(tt(region_restyle))(Replace rectangle styles while retaining character data.)
sitem(tt(region_copy))(Bounded opaque copies of retained rectangles.)
sitem(tt(transparent_copy))(Bounded region copying with plain spaces as transparent holes.)
sitem(tt(region_fill))(Styled rectangle fills using single-column tiles.)
sitem(tt(window_snapshots))(Bounded capture of retained window cells into an association.)
sitem(tt(cell_occupancy))(Optional conservative cell occupancy and style metadata in snapshots.)
sitem(tt(cell_inspection))(Structured readback of the current retained cell.)
sitem(tt(wide_cell_inspection))(Complete stored complex-character text, including combining marks.)
sitem(tt(colorinfo))(Runtime color information through tt(colorinfo).)
sitem(tt(resource_info))(Passive resource counts, budgets and prepared-row reuse
through tt(resourceinfo).)
sitem(tt(custom_borders))(The eight-character form of tt(border), including
printable ASCII borders.)
sitem(tt(default_colors))(Support for the color name tt(default) through
tt(use_default_colors).)
sitem(tt(geometry))(Support for the tt(geometry) subcommand's terminal size
query through tt(TIOCGWINSZ).)
sitem(tt(mouse))(Support for ncurses mouse input and the tt(mouse) subcommand.)
sitem(tt(resize))(Support for the tt(resize) subcommand through tt(resize_term).)
sitem(tt(wide_borders))(Non-ASCII border characters through wide curses.)
sitem(tt(truecolor))(Optional ncurses extended-color functions and terminfo queries for RGB.)
sitem(tt(structured_events))(Associative input records through the curses decoder.)
sitem(tt(streaming_paste))(Opt-in bracketed paste through curses, in bounded byte chunks.)
sitem(tt(suspend_resume))(Retained session handoff to foreground commands.)
sitem(tt(event_poll))(Per-call zero initial input timeout.)
sitem(tt(input_info))(Input descriptor, polling guidance and session input state.)
sitem(tt(capability_evidence))(Passive support/evidence/state records and record-only overrides.)
sitem(tt(capability_queries))(Opt-in bounded DEC/kitty requests and exact reply decoding.)
sitem(tt(synchronized_output))(Evidence-gated mode-2026 markers around explicit presentation.)
sitem(tt(focus_events))(Opt-in terminal focus reports with explicit mode ownership.)
sitem(tt(keyboard_events))(Negotiated kitty keyboard subset with bounded event decoding.)
sitem(tt(input_delay))(Explicit ncurses escape delay with end/unload restoration.)
sitem(tt(norefresh_events))(Opt-in event input without refreshing drawing windows,
currently with ncurses.)
sitem(tt(wide_events))(Locale-based wide-character input rather than raw bytes.)
sitem(tt(resize_events))(Terminal-size query or curses resize notification support.)
sitem(tt(prepared_rows))(Immutable session-scoped styled rows with clipping and inspection.)
sitem(tt(styled_spans))(Single-row styled text batching.)
sitem(tt(clipped_spans))(Styled-span drawing with one column budget.)
sitem(tt(textinfo))(Headless text measurement and prefix clipping.)
sitem(tt(text_wrapping))(Bounded headless column wrapping with original source ranges.)
sitem(tt(grapheme_boundaries))(Optional Unicode 17.0.0 terminal grapheme profile
for tt(textinfo) and tt(textpos); requires UTF-8 and tt(MULTIBYTE) at runtime.)
sitem(tt(text_policy))(Headless width-policy discovery.)
sitem(tt(grapheme_safe_text))(Optional shared native-width grapheme-safe queries and drawing.)
sitem(tt(text_positions))(Headless mapping between byte offsets and displayed columns.)
sitem(tt(wide_text))(Locale-based multibyte measurement and clipping, independent
of wide curses support.)
sitem(tt(wide_spans))(Wide characters and representable combining sequences in spans.)
endsitem()

A listed feature indicates compiled support, not that an operation will
succeed with the current terminal.  An omitted name from the list above
indicates that support was not compiled in.  This array is not a list of all
subcommands, terminfo capabilities, or enabled terminal protocols.  Its order
is unspecified and future versions may add names; callers should test for
individual names and ignore unfamiliar ones.  It is not an ABI identifier.

After loading the module, `tt(zmodload -F -e zdraw +p:zdraw_features)'
tests whether this parameter is provided and enabled.  This also works with
older modules which lack the parameter, returning status 1 without invoking
an unsupported tt(zdraw) subcommand.  Failure of this test means feature
information is unavailable; it does not establish that an operation is absent.
)
vindex(ZDRAW_COLORS)
item(tt(ZDRAW_COLORS))(
Readonly integer.  The maximum number of colors the terminal
supports.  This value is initialised by the curses library and is not
available until the first time tt(zdraw init) is run.
)
vindex(ZDRAW_COLOR_PAIRS)
item(tt(ZDRAW_COLOR_PAIRS))(
Readonly integer.  The maximum number of color pairs
var(fg_col)tt(/)var(bg_col) that may be defined in `tt(zdraw attr)'
commands; note this limit applies to all color pairs that have been
used whether or not they are currently active.  This value is initialised
by the curses library and is not available until the first time tt(zdraw
init) is run.
)
vindex(zdraw_attrs)
item(tt(zdraw_attrs))(
Readonly array.  The attributes supported by tt(zdraw); available
as soon as the module is loaded.
)
vindex(zdraw_colors)
item(tt(zdraw_colors))(
Readonly array.  The colors supported by tt(zdraw); available
as soon as the module is loaded.
)
vindex(zdraw_keycodes)
item(tt(zdraw_keycodes))(
Readonly array.  The values that may be returned in the second
parameter supplied to `tt(zdraw input)' in the order in which they
are defined internally by curses.  Not all function keys
are listed, only tt(F0); curses reserves space for tt(F0) up to tt(F63).
)
vindex(zdraw_windows)
item(tt(zdraw_windows))(
Readonly array. The current registered surfaces: tt(stdscr), ordinary windows
created with tt(addwin), and offscreen pads created with tt(addpad), until
deleted or released by tt(end).
)
enditem()
